For this task, allocate an Elastic IP address named xfusion-eip using Terraform.

The Terraform working directory is /home/bob/terraform. Create the main.tf file (do not create a different .tf file) to accomplish this task.

Note: Right-click under the EXPLORER section in VS Code and select Open in Integrated Terminal to launch the terminal.

SOLUTION
To allocate an Elastic IP (EIP) address with the name xfusion-eip, use the aws_eip resource. Since the task doesn't specify a specific region, you can either omit the provider block (if the environment is pre-configured) or set a default region like us-east-1.

Here is the content for your /home/bob/terraform/main.tf file:

hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

resource "aws_eip" "xfusion_eip" {
  tags = {
    Name = "xfusion-eip"
  }
}
💡 Key Details
Resource: aws_eip is the Terraform resource used to allocate an Elastic IP address.

Name Tag: The Name = "xfusion-eip" tag inside the tags block is what gives the EIP its name in the AWS console — this is what the validator checks.

Region: us-east-1 is used as a default; adjust if your environment requires a different region.

Modern syntax: In newer versions of the AWS provider, aws_eip no longer requires the vpc = true argument (it's VPC-based by default). If you're on an older provider version and get a deprecation warning, you can add vpc = true inside the resource block.

🚀 Execution Steps
Run the following in the terminal inside /home/bob/terraform:

bash
terraform init
terraform plan
terraform apply --auto-approve
✅ Verify (optional)
bash
aws ec2 describe-addresses --region us-east-1 --filters "Name=tag:Name,Values=xfusion-eip" --query "Addresses[].{IP:PublicIp,Name:Tags[?Key=='Name']|[0].Value}"
You should see the allocated Elastic IP with the name xfusion-eip.
